  "Question": "If \\(f(x)=x^{x+1}(x+2)^{x+3},\\) then \\[ \\begin{gathered} f(0)+f(-1)\\\\ {}+f(-2)+f(-3)= \\end{gathered} \\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\frac89\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac89\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{10}9\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Substitute each input before evaluating the powers. For a nonzero base \\(u\\), use \\(u^0=1\\) and \\(u^{-n}=1/u^n\\), where \\(n\\) is a positive integer. This gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(0)&=0^1\\cdot2^3=0,\\\\\nf(-1)&=(-1)^0\\cdot1^2=1,\\\\\nf(-2)&=(-2)^{-1}\\cdot0^1=0,\\\\\nf(-3)&=(-3)^{-2}\\cdot(-1)^0=\\frac19.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe zero bases have positive exponents, and the zero or negative exponents have nonzero bases, so every value is defined. Hence\n\\[\nf(0)+f(-1)+f(-2)+f(-3)\n=0+1+0+\\frac19=\\frac{10}{9}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\frac{10}{9}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
